    <string name="robotics_dof_m">DOF M</string>
    <string name="robotics_grashof_yes">Grashof holds: the shortest link can turn all the way round.</string>
    <string name="robotics_grashof_no">Not Grashof: no link turns all the way; they all rock.</string>
    <string name="robotics_m_no_ground">Without ground it floats in the plane. Fix at least one node with the Fixed tool.</string>
    <string name="robotics_m_negative">M = %1$d: an overconstrained structure, %2$d constraints too many. It doesn\'t move.</string>
    <string name="robotics_m_zero">M = 0: it is a structure. Drag a node with Move: it snaps back.</string>
    <string name="robotics_m_one">M = 1: one motor sets the position of everything. Put the motor on a grounded bar and press Animate.</string>
    <string name="robotics_m_many">M = %1$d: it needs %2$d motors. With only one, the rest hangs loose: drag a node to see it.</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_1">1. The ground</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_1_body">Everything fixed to the ground is a single link, number 1, even if it shows up here at %1$d pins. They are highlighted in the drawing.</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_1_fixed">A bar between two fixed pins doesn\'t move: it is part of the ground too.</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_2">2. The links</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_2_body">Count each moving rigid body: %1$d bars or plates and %2$d slider blocks. With the ground, N = %3$d. Each one carries its number in the drawing.</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_2_plates">There are %1$d plates (ternary links or more): even though they join 3 or more joints, each is ONE link.</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_3">3. The pairs</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_3_body">At each joint where k links meet there are k − 1 pairs. The badge on each node in the drawing is how many pairs it counts.</string>
    <string name="robotics_pair_simple">%1$s: joins links %2$s → 1 revolute pair.</string>
    <string name="robotics_pair_multiple">%1$s: %2$d links meet (%3$s) → %4$d revolute pairs (multiple joint).</string>
    <string name="robotics_pair_slider">%1$s: the slider block (link %2$d) slides on the ground → 1 prismatic pair.</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_4">4. The formula</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_4_body">Each moving link adds 3 DOF in the plane and each 1-DOF pair removes 2.</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_5">5. What does it mean?</string>
    <string name="robotics_guide_5_spatial">Counted as a spatial mechanism it gives another number: its parallel axes make some constraints redundant. That is why planar mechanisms use the planar formula.</string>
    <string name="robotics_quiz_title">Test your eye</string>
    <string name="robotics_quiz_hint">Count before looking: how many links, pairs and DOF does the drawing have? You can move it to help yourself.</string>
    <string name="robotics_quiz_check">Check</string>
    <string name="robotics_quiz_new">Another one</string>
    <string name="robotics_quiz_ok">Exactly! N = %1$d, j₁ = %2$d, M = %3$d.</string>
    <string name="robotics_quiz_wrong">Almost. Check %1$s. Open “Teach me to count” to see it step by step.</string>

